On Mon, Apr 8, 2013 at 3:52 AM, la .lindar. <lindarthebard@gmail.com> wrote:

> This is at least the third time I've been in a thread just like this since
> I started here, so can we please pin/sticky/do something to prevent this
> thread from reappearing? It's {lo}. It's already there. It's built into the
> word. I'm pretty sure I settled on this with Robin, though nobody
> remembers[1]. Just saying {lo gerku ku} is like "any/some dog(s)"[2]. In
> fact, you can't not say "any" or "some"[3]. That's just how the word works.
>
> <sarcasm>
> Sources:
> 1. This is me being petty. =D
> 2. Somewhere in the CLL. Probably the part where it explains that.
> 3. Try to prove otherwise. =P
> </sarcasm>
>

You can't quote the CLL about lo--xorlo aside, the CLL's lo is defined in
terms of da, which means that {lo gerku cu se tuple vo da} means "there
exists a dog has 4 legs", not "any dog has 4 legs" under CLL-lo. Under
xorlo as I understand it right now it's more like you can't not say "the
generic".

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

